WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:08.810
&gt;&gt; It's a true honor to have
Mike Jordan with us today.

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:11.140
Mike is a professor in

00:00:11.141 --> 00:00:13.030
the Department of
EECS and Statistics

00:00:13.031 --> 00:00:14.785
at UC Berkeley and he's been,

00:00:14.786 --> 00:00:16.090
obviously as many of you know,

00:00:16.091 --> 00:00:17.590
an energetic leader, I'd say,

00:00:17.591 --> 00:00:19.570
at the intersection of statistics

00:00:19.571 --> 00:00:22.780
and the visions put forth by

00:00:22.781 --> 00:00:25.420
the founders of the areas
of AI and cybernetics

00:00:25.421 --> 00:00:26.680
in terms of the possibilities

00:00:26.681 --> 00:00:28.750
for computational intelligence.

00:00:28.751 --> 00:00:32.410
He's worked in both theory and
in practical applications.

00:00:32.411 --> 00:00:34.060
Some of the practical
work includes his efforts

00:00:34.061 --> 00:00:37.315
in genetics and
signal processing,

00:00:37.316 --> 00:00:39.870
information retrieval, we
all know about some of

00:00:39.871 --> 00:00:44.265
the work there, and
natural language.

00:00:44.266 --> 00:00:45.570
On the technical side, he's

00:00:45.571 --> 00:00:48.090
made significant contributions

00:00:48.091 --> 00:00:49.935
in probabilistic
graphical models,

00:00:49.936 --> 00:00:52.920
Bayesian nonparametric
analyses, spectral methods,

00:00:52.921 --> 00:00:55.665
kernel machines and applications

00:00:55.666 --> 00:00:59.325
to distributing computing
systems among other areas.

00:00:59.326 --> 00:01:01.080
Speaking of probabilistic
graphical models,

00:01:01.081 --> 00:01:05.955
I have to say that
I first met Mike,

00:01:05.956 --> 00:01:08.280
I was starting to think of
that when it was, it was

00:01:08.281 --> 00:01:09.795
a great salient memory.

00:01:09.796 --> 00:01:11.615
&gt;&gt; We both didn't have
gray hair at the time.

00:01:11.616 --> 00:01:13.520
&gt;&gt; That's for sure. Mine turned

00:01:13.521 --> 00:01:15.860
gray a couple of months ago,
I don't know about you.

00:01:15.861 --> 00:01:18.994
But it was in Corvallis,

00:01:18.995 --> 00:01:21.110
there was a a Bayesian

00:01:21.111 --> 00:01:23.660
probabilistic graphical
model summer school

00:01:23.661 --> 00:01:28.180
in Corvallis that
[inaudible] together.

00:01:28.181 --> 00:01:31.305
Right. I taught one course.

00:01:31.306 --> 00:01:32.550
But Mike was with
a student and we were

00:01:32.551 --> 00:01:33.960
walking out at break,

00:01:33.961 --> 00:01:35.280
it was a morning break,

00:01:35.281 --> 00:01:39.050
and Michael was the most
engaged student in the room.

00:01:39.051 --> 00:01:40.640
He walked out muttering,

00:01:40.641 --> 00:01:42.470
'This stuff is incredible.

00:01:42.471 --> 00:01:44.130
This stuff is incredible.'

00:01:44.131 --> 00:01:45.540
I remember he said that,
'These probabilistic

00:01:45.541 --> 00:01:47.085
graphical models are incredible.'

00:01:47.086 --> 00:01:49.514
I guess it was one of
your early exposures,

00:01:49.515 --> 00:01:53.535
but you were just like dry
kindling waiting to be

00:01:53.536 --> 00:01:55.695
lit on fire at that moment

00:01:55.696 --> 00:01:58.545
as he was outraged
to see that face.

00:01:58.546 --> 00:02:00.420
Another comment he
once mentioned to me,

00:02:00.421 --> 00:02:01.620
and maybe you can tell the group

00:02:01.621 --> 00:02:03.255
this sexual story, he said that,

00:02:03.256 --> 00:02:05.984
'You were doing X,'
and I think it was,

00:02:05.985 --> 00:02:07.890
maybe it was stat somewhere,

00:02:07.891 --> 00:02:10.650
and 'You had this
chance to go hang

00:02:10.651 --> 00:02:14.190
out with Don Norman and
Ruppman and Dave Rumelhart.'

00:02:14.191 --> 00:02:15.630
The picture you painted,

00:02:15.631 --> 00:02:16.755
I think it was over
dinner one night,

00:02:16.756 --> 00:02:19.245
is you were on

00:02:19.246 --> 00:02:23.355
this out-of-control kayak
going over a waterfall and you

00:02:23.356 --> 00:02:24.480
jumped and you grabbed

00:02:24.481 --> 00:02:28.860
the branch to stop

00:02:28.861 --> 00:02:31.140
what you were doing and
go in a new direction.

00:02:31.141 --> 00:02:33.060
That's exactly how you
put it so maybe you

00:02:33.061 --> 00:02:35.555
can tell us more about
my memory there.

00:02:35.556 --> 00:02:38.610
Anyway, it's really
fabulous to have you here.

00:02:38.611 --> 00:02:40.380
I didn't finish
your whole bio here

00:02:40.381 --> 00:02:42.480
but let me just say
that Michael has

00:02:42.481 --> 00:02:46.260
received many well-deserved
recognitions,

00:02:46.261 --> 00:02:48.330
you can read about them
in the bio online.

00:02:48.331 --> 00:02:50.850
Today, Mike's talking about
gradient-based optimization,

00:02:50.851 --> 00:02:53.070
accelerated, non-convex,
and stochastic.

00:02:53.071 --> 00:02:54.720
Take it away. Thanks.

00:02:54.721 --> 00:03:00.930
&gt;&gt; All right. That maybe

00:03:00.931 --> 00:03:04.090
the most idiosyncratic
introduction I've ever had.

00:03:04.490 --> 00:03:06.840
I wish you could
just keep on going,

00:03:06.841 --> 00:03:09.015
it was very entertaining.

00:03:09.016 --> 00:03:11.895
So, I'm just going to dive in.

00:03:11.896 --> 00:03:15.390
I'm a technical researcher
academic and I just work on

00:03:15.391 --> 00:03:19.095
the next wave of things and
all of this buzz and hype,

00:03:19.096 --> 00:03:22.020
I'm a little bit
problematic here.

00:03:22.021 --> 00:03:24.105
It's hard to avoid,

00:03:24.106 --> 00:03:26.330
but I did build
a little cottage in my garden

00:03:26.331 --> 00:03:27.440
and there's no
Internet down there

00:03:27.441 --> 00:03:28.820
so I mostly can avoid it.

00:03:28.821 --> 00:03:31.800
But I was recently in Chin-
about a year ago in China,

00:03:31.801 --> 00:03:35.960
and I'm in the men's room
and the people

00:03:35.961 --> 00:03:36.980
behind me were talking about deep

00:03:36.981 --> 00:03:38.240
learning in the
men's room and then,

00:03:38.241 --> 00:03:41.240
in a random restaurant in China
and I'm going, 'Oh, man.'

00:03:41.241 --> 00:03:43.640
I finally, this week, wrote
a book, the last week,

00:03:43.641 --> 00:03:45.320
I wrote a little blog thing

00:03:45.321 --> 00:03:48.695
to put up a few thoughts out
there, just laid them down.

00:03:48.696 --> 00:03:51.050
I'm not on social media
at all but apparently,

00:03:51.051 --> 00:03:52.760
it's what you call viral
so it was really a lot.

00:03:52.761 --> 00:03:57.265
I will not just jump into
most of my interests.

00:03:57.266 --> 00:03:58.890
I'm going to say
a little bit about

00:03:58.891 --> 00:04:03.675
this broader picture
and obviously,

00:04:03.676 --> 00:04:05.430
a lot of you know probably
even more about AI.

00:04:05.431 --> 00:04:07.214
I'm not an AI researcher,
I'm a statistician,

00:04:07.215 --> 00:04:09.180
so many of you,

00:04:09.181 --> 00:04:10.440
with apologies, will
know more than me.

00:04:10.441 --> 00:04:13.860
But I do think that
this word is unfortunate.

00:04:13.861 --> 00:04:18.330
I think it's a great aspiration,
I think inherent to our

00:04:18.331 --> 00:04:21.300
students. It was the aspiration
that we had computers

00:04:21.301 --> 00:04:24.450
but can they be made to seem
intelligent in some way,

00:04:24.451 --> 00:04:26.400
and I think it's still
an industry of aspiration.

00:04:26.401 --> 00:04:28.760
Someone turn on the gain
a little bit here.

00:04:28.761 --> 00:04:33.205
I'm getting feedback.
Am I only hearing it?

00:04:33.206 --> 00:04:33.940
&gt;&gt; It's resonating.

00:04:33.941 --> 00:04:37.130
&gt;&gt; It was resonating
high frequencies.

00:04:37.131 --> 00:04:39.750
So, we don't have it
yet. We don't have it.

00:04:39.751 --> 00:04:41.350
We don't know what
intelligence is, I think,

00:04:41.351 --> 00:04:43.210
and all these neural nets are
things that are just taking

00:04:43.211 --> 00:04:45.650
the data, mimicking intelligent.

00:04:45.651 --> 00:04:47.810
I don't know if we're going
to have it anytime soon.

00:04:47.811 --> 00:04:49.985
We don't need it, really,
for most things that-

00:04:49.986 --> 00:04:51.760
So many cool things
we can do without

00:04:51.761 --> 00:04:53.515
trying to figure out what it is.

00:04:53.516 --> 00:04:56.055
Also, a lot of the
people who work on,

00:04:56.056 --> 00:04:58.355
use the AI buzzword these days
where they used to learn

00:04:58.356 --> 00:05:00.550
autonomous systems because then,

00:05:00.551 --> 00:05:02.975
it's really intelligent,

00:05:02.976 --> 00:05:04.325
because no one's
telling it what to do.

00:05:04.326 --> 00:05:06.765
I think that's fine but it's
somewhat misguided, too.

00:05:06.766 --> 00:05:09.130
Really, all of our toasters
should be networked together

00:05:09.131 --> 00:05:12.520
and decisions should be
made not independently.

00:05:12.521 --> 00:05:15.295
They should be made hard as
other decisions, and so on.

00:05:15.296 --> 00:05:19.795
So, I think that AI is used
too much as just a wildcard,

00:05:19.796 --> 00:05:22.040
you throw it in
whenever you need to.

00:05:22.041 --> 00:05:24.260
I don't also watch television but

00:05:24.261 --> 00:05:25.910
I gather that
Mark Zuckerberg went for

00:05:25.911 --> 00:05:28.550
a Congress recently and I

00:05:28.551 --> 00:05:30.005
gather that whenever he was asked

00:05:30.006 --> 00:05:31.960
any kind of a
challenging question,

00:05:31.961 --> 00:05:33.650
he'd say, 'Well, AI
will handle that.'

00:05:33.651 --> 00:05:36.600
Apparently, I heard it
16 times in his talk.

00:05:36.680 --> 00:05:39.420
So, there's a little
hubris there about

00:05:39.421 --> 00:05:41.550
computer science saying
we resolve it and also,

00:05:41.551 --> 00:05:44.625
let's break things and then
later fix them, and all that.

00:05:44.626 --> 00:05:48.150
But when you use AI as
an intellectual wildcard,

00:05:48.151 --> 00:05:49.440
that's just not responsible.

00:05:49.441 --> 00:05:51.510
I just don't think
that our community

00:05:51.511 --> 00:05:53.140
should be supporting that kind
of irresponsible dialogue.

00:05:53.141 --> 00:05:54.720
So, I wish we weren't saying AI.

00:05:54.721 --> 00:05:56.370
That's fine as an aspiration but,

00:05:56.371 --> 00:05:58.320
already a machine
learning bothered

00:05:58.321 --> 00:06:00.330
me because we had free area,

00:06:00.331 --> 00:06:03.705
called statistics,
which is the same stuff.

00:06:03.706 --> 00:06:07.390
That's so well, I lost
all those battles.

00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:10.185
Let me skip over that slide.

00:06:10.186 --> 00:06:12.780
So, in this blog, I said,

00:06:12.781 --> 00:06:15.125
'Let's don't use
AI for everything.

00:06:15.126 --> 00:06:17.475
Let's make some distinctions.'

00:06:17.476 --> 00:06:19.490
There's some historical
ones that are still

00:06:19.491 --> 00:06:21.560
relevant and then there's ones

00:06:21.561 --> 00:06:23.150
that have to do with
what problems we

00:06:23.151 --> 00:06:26.010
should be solving and
that's pretty clear on.

00:06:26.380 --> 00:06:29.565
Let's call it human-imitative AI,

00:06:29.566 --> 00:06:33.815
that's where it was
back in the origins.

00:06:33.816 --> 00:06:37.610
That's what I have toyed in mind.

00:06:37.611 --> 00:06:40.550
It's a great aspiration to
think about how do we get

00:06:40.551 --> 00:06:42.140
this thing that we
know has intelligence

00:06:42.141 --> 00:06:44.880
inside of a computer.

00:06:44.990 --> 00:06:49.335
But really, then
the next few decades

00:06:49.336 --> 00:06:51.150
came forward and I

00:06:51.151 --> 00:06:53.415
don't think very much
progress was made on that.

00:06:53.416 --> 00:06:56.730
We don't really have anything
like intelligence yet,

00:06:56.731 --> 00:06:57.930
but we did have lots of pattern

00:06:57.931 --> 00:06:59.040
recognition stuff and lots

00:06:59.041 --> 00:07:01.260
of information retrieval stuff

00:07:01.261 --> 00:07:03.015
and algorithms of all kinds.

00:07:03.016 --> 00:07:05.070
I think it's mostly IA,

00:07:05.071 --> 00:07:07.170
it's mostly augment
our intelligence.

00:07:07.171 --> 00:07:09.465
So, search engines
augment our intelligence,

00:07:09.466 --> 00:07:12.270
translation systems do,
and so on so forth.

00:07:12.271 --> 00:07:14.130
Interacting with humans and not

00:07:14.131 --> 00:07:16.230
replacing human
intelligence but making

00:07:16.231 --> 00:07:17.940
it somehow more interesting and

00:07:17.941 --> 00:07:20.475
more deep, that's
what's happened.

00:07:20.476 --> 00:07:22.380
But also, I think what's happened

00:07:22.381 --> 00:07:23.640
is this thing that's
often called Internet

00:07:23.641 --> 00:07:26.385
of Things by people
from computer science.

00:07:26.386 --> 00:07:28.455
That's just a limited
version of it.

00:07:28.456 --> 00:07:30.090
The real bigger version
is the one that I think

00:07:30.091 --> 00:07:31.755
most of us are used to, is that,

00:07:31.756 --> 00:07:33.870
all around us, there's
a network of intelligence

00:07:33.871 --> 00:07:36.240
where the system
behaves intelligently.

00:07:36.241 --> 00:07:37.860
It gives us options
for transportation,

00:07:37.861 --> 00:07:39.180
it gives us options
for medical care,

00:07:39.181 --> 00:07:40.860
it gives us options for
financial services,

00:07:40.861 --> 00:07:42.030
commerce and so on.

00:07:42.031 --> 00:07:43.410
We want that overall system to

00:07:43.411 --> 00:07:44.850
behave intelligently
and to do that,

00:07:44.851 --> 00:07:46.320
you don't need
the individual nodes to be

00:07:46.321 --> 00:07:47.995
intelligent agents at all.

00:07:47.996 --> 00:07:49.770
You want the overall system
to be intelligent in

00:07:49.771 --> 00:07:52.590
the context of
human aspirations and goals.

00:07:52.591 --> 00:07:55.480
So, if we think about that,

00:07:55.481 --> 00:07:58.830
this focus on vision and speech,

00:07:58.831 --> 00:08:01.670
and NLP and robotics is

00:08:01.671 --> 00:08:03.110
misplaced because you don't

00:08:03.111 --> 00:08:05.940
need those for
these brand or goals.

00:08:07.040 --> 00:08:09.150
Those are hard problems,

00:08:09.151 --> 00:08:10.490
they're not solved at all to

00:08:10.491 --> 00:08:12.560
this day and focusing on
one of them makes you not

00:08:12.561 --> 00:08:14.030
focus on many of
the other problems

00:08:14.031 --> 00:08:16.095
that are really important.

00:08:16.096 --> 00:08:17.220
I just wrote down something I've

00:08:17.221 --> 00:08:19.020
been interested in working on.

00:08:19.021 --> 00:08:20.630
Some of them are classical AI

00:08:20.631 --> 00:08:22.160
aspirations but many
of them are not.

00:08:22.161 --> 00:08:23.510
So, I'm going to
talk a little bit

00:08:23.511 --> 00:08:25.475
about multiple decision making.

00:08:25.476 --> 00:08:28.945
If you're in the class
in the AI world,

00:08:28.946 --> 00:08:31.230
you think about an agent
making a decision.

00:08:31.231 --> 00:08:33.030
Then maybe you think
about a sequence of

00:08:33.031 --> 00:08:34.730
decisions, that's
the reinforcement executive.

00:08:34.731 --> 00:08:36.630
With the real world problems,

00:08:36.631 --> 00:08:38.610
you make 100,000
decisions simultaneously

00:08:38.611 --> 00:08:40.710
for the current medical city
or around the world,

00:08:40.711 --> 00:08:43.080
or for the transportation
network or whatever.

00:08:43.081 --> 00:08:45.165
How do you control
errors across those?

00:08:45.166 --> 00:08:46.470
You can't treat each decision

00:08:46.471 --> 00:08:49.245
independently and think of
about it as collective agents.

00:08:49.246 --> 00:08:50.550
Also, I'm really used in

00:08:50.551 --> 00:08:52.200
systems that don't
just have data flowing

00:08:52.201 --> 00:08:53.670
around but use that data flow to

00:08:53.671 --> 00:08:55.410
create markets and
to create value,

00:08:55.411 --> 00:08:57.885
and make people be able to
enter into markets and get

00:08:57.886 --> 00:08:59.070
things out of it other than

00:08:59.071 --> 00:09:01.870
just having a platform
that they go to.

00:09:01.871 --> 00:09:04.800
Uncertainty to this day is
still not talked about very

00:09:04.801 --> 00:09:07.500
well by the
machine-learning people.

00:09:07.501 --> 00:09:08.670
They don't seem to care

00:09:08.671 --> 00:09:09.630
about it because they
just think there's

00:09:09.631 --> 00:09:10.920
enough data that
there's no uncertainty,

00:09:10.921 --> 00:09:11.865
and that's just wrong.

00:09:11.866 --> 00:09:13.245
Because they didn't think about

00:09:13.246 --> 00:09:15.510
domains were searched
or commerce,

00:09:15.511 --> 00:09:17.250
where if you get
the wrong answers,

00:09:17.251 --> 00:09:19.020
no one dies or no one's hurt,

00:09:19.021 --> 00:09:20.730
no one's really inconvenienced.

00:09:20.731 --> 00:09:22.755
But we're trying to roll
this out in worlds where

00:09:22.756 --> 00:09:25.050
people do die and get
hurt and inconvenienced,

00:09:25.051 --> 00:09:26.895
then uncertainty
matters critically.

00:09:26.896 --> 00:09:29.460
You tell me some machine
learning algorithm analyzed

00:09:29.461 --> 00:09:31.980
my genome and my current
physiological state

00:09:31.981 --> 00:09:33.715
and says I need to have
a heart operation,

00:09:33.716 --> 00:09:35.725
I'm going to say how
sure are you about that.

00:09:35.726 --> 00:09:38.020
Should we get some more data?
What if this happened?

00:09:38.021 --> 00:09:40.265
All the other things
around these systems.

00:09:40.266 --> 00:09:41.890
Cloud Edge
interactions, if you're

00:09:41.891 --> 00:09:43.400
going to really build something
like a self-driving car,

00:09:43.401 --> 00:09:44.840
you need to be able to have local

00:09:44.841 --> 00:09:46.640
intelligence that's fast and

00:09:46.641 --> 00:09:48.910
you need to go up to the Cloud
for more computation,

00:09:48.911 --> 00:09:49.930
you need to communicate.

00:09:49.931 --> 00:09:50.695
You need to fix all of

00:09:50.696 --> 00:09:52.430
the problems have
nothing to do with

00:09:52.431 --> 00:09:55.880
human intelligence
but are critical.

00:09:55.881 --> 00:09:57.530
Moreover, this
overall system will

00:09:57.531 --> 00:09:58.910
have different kinds
of knowledge in

00:09:58.911 --> 00:10:00.080
different cars and
it will have to get

00:10:00.081 --> 00:10:01.655
transmitted and made accord,

00:10:01.656 --> 00:10:03.505
those are the
interesting challenges.

00:10:03.506 --> 00:10:04.590
Anyway, I could go on.

00:10:04.591 --> 00:10:07.120
Provenance is one, the database
we will talk a lot about,

00:10:07.121 --> 00:10:08.620
but I think this is
really critical.

00:10:08.621 --> 00:10:11.300
You're going to measure data
at one place and one time

00:10:11.301 --> 00:10:12.800
in one situation and it needs to

00:10:12.801 --> 00:10:14.450
be used in another place, time,

00:10:14.451 --> 00:10:16.070
and situation, and you've got to

00:10:16.071 --> 00:10:17.780
record some information
that makes it

00:10:17.781 --> 00:10:19.220
possible to move it from

00:10:19.221 --> 00:10:20.030
one place another and have

00:10:20.031 --> 00:10:21.980
some reliability
about doing that.

00:10:21.981 --> 00:10:23.810
So, a lot of

00:10:23.811 --> 00:10:25.430
my e-mail colleagues just
say, 'Well, that's boring.

00:10:25.431 --> 00:10:26.510
That's not the exciting.'

00:10:26.511 --> 00:10:28.615
Well, no, that was not boring.

00:10:28.616 --> 00:10:30.250
That's what database people did,

00:10:30.251 --> 00:10:31.610
they changed
the world by building

