So, if you take
a little two-by-two table

00:12:25.161 --> 00:12:28.380
of classification table, 0101,

00:12:28.381 --> 00:12:30.975
there's a certain ratio in that,

00:12:30.976 --> 00:12:35.610
that's called the precision,
another one's called recall,

00:12:35.611 --> 00:12:37.610
and other one is accuracy.

00:12:37.611 --> 00:12:39.580
There's also another one, it's

00:12:39.581 --> 00:12:41.105
called FDR, just in
that little table.

00:12:41.106 --> 00:12:42.670
It's not a brand new
thing out of it.

00:12:42.671 --> 00:12:44.035
But it happens to
be one that you can

00:12:44.036 --> 00:12:45.475
aggregate across
multiple decisions and

00:12:45.476 --> 00:12:49.180
get control over the overall
fractions of errors in that.

00:12:50.190 --> 00:12:52.780
So, can you control FDR?

00:12:52.781 --> 00:12:54.435
Well, starting with
Benjamini-Hochberg,

00:12:54.436 --> 00:12:58.545
there was a line of research
that's been doing that,

