these assumptions and this
is piece of publicity.

00:14:08.436 --> 00:14:09.890
I don't want to get
this a separate talk,

00:14:09.891 --> 00:14:12.980
but we've just written a paper
which I think is kind of

00:14:12.981 --> 00:14:14.715
a very full version of
this you can now have

00:14:14.716 --> 00:14:17.580
hypotheses or related by
a Directed Acyclic Graph.

00:14:17.581 --> 00:14:19.480
So, we if this
hypothesis is true,

00:14:19.481 --> 00:14:21.445
this must be or if there's
a logical constraints.

00:14:21.446 --> 00:14:23.625
So if reject this one,
you must reject this one.

00:14:23.626 --> 00:14:25.015
That's been allowed now.

00:14:25.016 --> 00:14:26.895
More of it can be done
on an online matter,

00:14:26.896 --> 00:14:29.335
that you can start rejecting
hypotheses or accepting.

00:14:29.336 --> 00:14:31.740
Over time, I can stop
you at any moment and

00:14:31.741 --> 00:14:32.980
your error fraction up

00:14:32.981 --> 00:14:34.640
to that moment should
be under control.

00:14:34.641 --> 00:14:36.035
So, there's a simple relevant,

00:14:36.036 --> 00:14:38.050
simple algorithm that does
these kind of things.

00:14:38.051 --> 00:14:41.640
Why am I holding this? All right.

00:14:41.641 --> 00:14:43.280
But I also want to focus
on another side of

00:14:43.281 --> 00:14:45.910
multiple decisions
as less discussed.

00:14:45.911 --> 00:14:48.050
It is about load balancing,

00:14:48.051 --> 00:14:49.420
and this is where economic ideas

00:14:49.421 --> 00:14:51.440
I think are coming into play.

00:14:51.441 --> 00:14:54.310
So, think about situations

00:14:54.311 --> 00:14:55.920
where we're making
100,000 decisions,

00:14:55.921 --> 00:14:59.780
like in a transportation system
or a medical system.

00:14:59.781 --> 00:15:01.580
Now, if there's no
scarcity of resources,

00:15:01.581 --> 00:15:02.920
it's not such a big deal.

00:15:02.921 --> 00:15:04.225
But in real world, there's almost

00:15:04.226 --> 00:15:05.775
always scarcity of resources.

00:15:05.776 --> 00:15:08.720
So, let's think about
a recommendation system.

00:15:08.721 --> 00:15:11.075
Amazon built one. You come in

00:15:11.076 --> 00:15:12.850
and it recommends
maybe a movie to you,

00:15:12.851 --> 00:15:14.640
and it does that by looking
at all the features and notes

00:15:14.641 --> 00:15:16.690
about you and so on.

00:15:16.691 --> 00:15:18.350
Now later that day,

00:15:18.351 --> 00:15:19.700
someone else comes on the system

00:15:19.701 --> 00:15:21.530
and a recommendation is
made to that person.

00:15:21.531 --> 00:15:23.780
Those are independently
done, right?

00:15:23.781 --> 00:15:25.210
Suppose it's the case

00:15:25.211 --> 00:15:27.610
that you recommend
the same movie to everybody.

00:15:27.611 --> 00:15:29.240
A hundred thousand people
came to your site,

00:15:29.241 --> 00:15:31.765
99,000 got a certain
movie recommendation.

00:15:31.766 --> 00:15:33.290
That's fine. There's no scarcity.

00:15:33.291 --> 00:15:34.950
I can stream the same bits
to everybody.

00:15:34.951 --> 00:15:36.170
All right? What if I recommend

00:15:36.171 --> 00:15:37.330
the same book to everybody?

00:15:37.331 --> 00:15:39.020
Well, it's a little bit
of a problem.

00:15:39.021 --> 00:15:40.400
If 99,000 people order it,

00:15:40.401 --> 00:15:42.180
it's going to be
a little bit of a problem.

00:15:42.181 --> 00:15:43.900
But we're not yet in
the core of the problem.

00:15:43.901 --> 00:15:45.200
The core of the problem is
something more like this.

00:15:45.201 --> 00:15:47.485
What if I recommend the
same restaurant to everyone?

00:15:47.486 --> 00:15:49.590
So tonight in Redmond,

00:15:49.591 --> 00:15:50.860
if you all go out,

00:15:50.861 --> 00:15:52.410
and your app says,

00:15:52.411 --> 00:15:54.445
"We recommend this restaurant",

00:15:54.446 --> 00:15:56.285
based on your profile
and all that,

00:15:56.286 --> 00:15:58.475
my Poisson statistics, there's
going to be a good chance

00:15:58.476 --> 00:16:01.265
that 400 people get
recommended same restaurant.

00:16:01.266 --> 00:16:02.680
You although there, and

00:16:02.681 --> 00:16:03.845
there's a line and
no one's happy.

00:16:03.846 --> 00:16:05.960
No one's getting served.
How do you fix that?

00:16:05.961 --> 00:16:09.395
Well, if you're a computer
science [inaudible] you say,

00:16:09.396 --> 00:16:12.820
Zuckerberg-type person, you say,

00:16:12.821 --> 00:16:15.480
well, we're just
better algorithms, AI.

00:16:15.710 --> 00:16:19.670
Yeah. Well, no. That's
not going to really work.

00:16:19.671 --> 00:16:20.595
We're not going to
be able to know how

00:16:20.596 --> 00:16:21.670
to do that load balancing.

00:16:21.671 --> 00:16:23.960
There's all kinds of
issues that would arise.

00:16:23.961 --> 00:16:26.440
What if we figure out
the best path from

00:16:26.441 --> 00:16:29.240
here to the airport and
we recommend that to you,

00:16:29.241 --> 00:16:30.875
and to you, and to you, right?

00:16:30.876 --> 00:16:33.335
Well, everybody are going on
the same street. All right?

00:16:33.336 --> 00:16:35.305
Again, you might think, no
big deal. We can fix that.

00:16:35.306 --> 00:16:36.890
Those are small problems,

00:16:36.891 --> 00:16:38.675
right? But they really are.

00:16:38.676 --> 00:16:41.225
So now, think about building
a financial service

00:16:41.226 --> 00:16:42.530
and I'm going to roll
it out say in China

00:16:42.531 --> 00:16:44.650
to 500 million people.

00:16:44.820 --> 00:16:47.180
Because people have some
money and I'm going to

00:16:47.181 --> 00:16:48.665
make recommendations to them.

00:16:48.666 --> 00:16:50.990
This financial, everybody
should buy that stock.

00:16:50.991 --> 00:16:53.020
Right? By Poisson statistics,

00:16:53.021 --> 00:16:54.360
there's a good chance
I'm gonna recommend

00:16:54.361 --> 00:16:55.920
a 100,000 people to
buy certain stock.

00:16:55.921 --> 00:16:57.785
I'm going to
destabilize the market.

00:16:57.786 --> 00:17:00.390
These aren't cartoons.
You can see that this

00:17:00.391 --> 00:17:02.715
is really the core
of the problem.

00:17:02.716 --> 00:17:04.835
Okay. So now, how
do you fix this?

00:17:04.836 --> 00:17:05.990
Well, you bring in economics.

00:17:05.991 --> 00:17:07.910
You don't just treat this
as a platform that you

00:17:07.911 --> 00:17:09.985
build and do machine
learning on. All right?

00:17:09.986 --> 00:17:12.280
You recognize that whenever
you made data-flow,

00:17:12.281 --> 00:17:15.335
you can build on top of
that economic ideas. All right?

00:17:15.336 --> 00:17:17.485
So, think about
the restaurant situation,

00:17:17.486 --> 00:17:20.895
what you really need is
a two-way market, okay?

00:17:20.896 --> 00:17:22.100
So you need that the people in

00:17:22.101 --> 00:17:23.495
the restaurants have one app

00:17:23.496 --> 00:17:24.990
and the people who
are the customers

00:17:24.991 --> 00:17:26.500
have a different app, right?

00:17:26.501 --> 00:17:28.325
The people on restaurants
say, "Tonight,

00:17:28.326 --> 00:17:30.705
I have 50 seats open or
there's a wedding in there

00:17:30.706 --> 00:17:31.850
but I got 50 seats open and I

00:17:31.851 --> 00:17:33.440
prefer people from out of town,

00:17:33.441 --> 00:17:35.005
and I prefer
young people whatever."

00:17:35.006 --> 00:17:37.040
I have some policy, right?

00:17:37.041 --> 00:17:38.990
So now, when I walk
out of the movie at

00:17:38.991 --> 00:17:41.180
7:00 PM, my app says,

00:17:41.181 --> 00:17:43.590
that restaurant and knows
about you and it makes

00:17:43.591 --> 00:17:46.340
a 10 percent discount for
the next 10 minutes, all right?

00:17:46.341 --> 00:17:48.565
Now look, that's great. I accept.

00:17:48.566 --> 00:17:50.605
Now, the other app

00:17:50.606 --> 00:17:52.320
has field one of the seats
in the restaurant.

00:17:52.321 --> 00:17:53.875
There's now 49 seats left

00:17:53.876 --> 00:17:55.985
and it keeps going
until it's full.

00:17:55.986 --> 00:17:57.420
I mean, it's an open market.

00:17:57.421 --> 00:17:58.440
All the other restaurant see

00:17:58.441 --> 00:18:00.265
that that favorite
restaurant is now

00:18:00.266 --> 00:18:04.570
full and they start making
their own offers saying,

00:18:04.571 --> 00:18:06.600
big opportunity, I'll make
a 30 percent discount

00:18:06.601 --> 00:18:07.380
and I'll fill my restaurant

00:18:07.381 --> 00:18:08.665
tonight for the first time ever.

00:18:08.666 --> 00:18:10.355
All right? So now data,

00:18:10.356 --> 00:18:12.240
just by the connection of data,

00:18:12.241 --> 00:18:13.780
you're able to build
a two-way market.

00:18:13.781 --> 00:18:15.120
All right? You are ready to solve

00:18:15.121 --> 00:18:17.905
the load balancing problem
which has gone, okay?

00:18:17.906 --> 00:18:19.330
Plus, you'd be able
to make connections

00:18:19.331 --> 00:18:21.685
between business and
consumers directly,

00:18:21.686 --> 00:18:24.090
not between you as a
computer science platform

00:18:24.091 --> 00:18:25.815
and business or you

00:18:25.816 --> 00:18:27.765
and computer science platform
and the customer,

00:18:27.766 --> 00:18:30.605
directly between them, and
you've created a market.

00:18:30.606 --> 00:18:32.420
Now, if you want to take
your five percent cut,

00:18:32.421 --> 00:18:34.165
you can make money off of that.

00:18:34.166 --> 00:18:36.360
That's all good. All right?

00:18:36.361 --> 00:18:37.755
Now, here's another example.

00:18:37.756 --> 00:18:40.375
So I'm involved in a company
called United Masters.

00:18:40.376 --> 00:18:41.670
I'm on their scientific
advisory board.

00:18:41.671 --> 00:18:44.530
It's a fantastic [inaudible]
it's the people really in

00:18:44.531 --> 00:18:48.175
the music business but kind
of on the musician side.

00:18:48.176 --> 00:18:50.970
So, it's easier than ever to make

00:18:50.971 --> 00:18:52.170
music people were making

00:18:52.171 --> 00:18:53.730
really good music
on the weekends.

00:18:53.731 --> 00:18:55.120
This is not their full time job

00:18:55.121 --> 00:18:56.780
and upload it to SoundCloud.

00:18:56.781 --> 00:18:58.485
Then, the streaming services

00:18:58.486 --> 00:18:59.830
are getting that
music and people are

00:18:59.831 --> 00:19:01.455
listening to it more
than ever before

00:19:01.456 --> 00:19:02.700
but no money is flowing.

00:19:02.701 --> 00:19:03.980
No one's making
money off of that,

00:19:03.981 --> 00:19:05.470
and that's a problem because

00:19:05.471 --> 00:19:07.445
people can't have that as a job.

00:19:07.446 --> 00:19:09.425
There's a big loss of

00:19:09.426 --> 00:19:11.985
opportunity that a market
would be able to create.

00:19:11.986 --> 00:19:16.165
So, how do you fix that
while you create a market?

00:19:16.166 --> 00:19:18.970
All right. So,
this particular company

00:19:18.971 --> 00:19:20.270
is doing that in
the following way.

00:19:20.271 --> 00:19:23.260
They partner with the people
who have the data-flow.

00:19:23.261 --> 00:19:25.910
For any given budding musician,

00:19:25.911 --> 00:19:28.100
you're not the top 100
that the record companies

00:19:28.101 --> 00:19:30.630
had picked up on but the
next 100,000 or million.

00:19:30.631 --> 00:19:32.760
They take each one of
them in and they collect

00:19:32.761 --> 00:19:35.290
all the data of all the people
who've listening to them,

00:19:35.291 --> 00:19:37.160
and then they reveal that
to them on a dashboard.

00:19:37.161 --> 00:19:38.335
So all you can see a map of

00:19:38.336 --> 00:19:41.795
California and I would see
all the cities this week,

00:19:41.796 --> 00:19:43.545
how many people have
been listening to me

00:19:43.546 --> 00:19:45.715
or of the whole US? All right?

00:19:45.716 --> 00:19:46.730
A lot of these people,

00:19:46.731 --> 00:19:48.835
they have a big listenership.

00:19:48.836 --> 00:19:50.690
So if I learned for
example that for

00:19:50.691 --> 00:19:53.035
some reason in Orlando, Florida

00:19:53.036 --> 00:19:54.150
there's 10,000 people had

00:19:54.151 --> 00:19:55.565
been listening to
me this past week,

00:19:55.566 --> 00:19:57.795
somebody like them and
tell they're friends.

