00:21:08.226 --> 00:21:09.840
In sums, these are
really things that I'm

00:21:09.841 --> 00:21:11.350
currently interested
in working on.

00:21:11.351 --> 00:21:14.760
So, I don't have results
per se to talk about,

00:21:14.761 --> 00:21:19.505
but I'm quite convinced
the results are going to come.

00:21:19.506 --> 00:21:21.785
Let me talk a little bit about,

00:21:21.786 --> 00:21:25.665
now turn to the core of what
I promised to talk about.

00:21:25.666 --> 00:21:27.660
So, we're going to be linking

00:21:27.661 --> 00:21:29.415
statistics and computer science.

00:21:29.416 --> 00:21:31.320
So, I am really an academic.

00:21:31.321 --> 00:21:33.230
I don't want to just do
this in an ad hoc way

00:21:33.231 --> 00:21:35.360
and create something
that kind of works,

00:21:35.361 --> 00:21:37.610
but I want to understand
the fundamental principles.

00:21:37.611 --> 00:21:39.350
To go, I am particularly
in lower bounds

00:21:39.351 --> 00:21:40.450
more and more
these days because that

00:21:40.451 --> 00:21:42.030
strips away all the
inessential stuff

00:21:42.031 --> 00:21:43.985
and lets you focus
on the essentials.

00:21:43.986 --> 00:21:45.950
I don't think that
machine learning

00:21:45.951 --> 00:21:47.110
has achieved what
people think it's

00:21:47.111 --> 00:21:48.600
achieved that it
blended statistics

00:21:48.601 --> 00:21:50.250
and computer science
in some deep way.

00:21:50.251 --> 00:21:52.050
I think that borrowed
ideas from here,

00:21:52.051 --> 00:21:54.270
there kind of put them
together willy-nilly,

00:21:54.271 --> 00:21:56.740
but there's no
foundational principles

00:21:56.741 --> 00:21:58.670
that have emerged by
bringing those two together.

00:21:58.671 --> 00:22:00.660
We're missing those
foundational principles.

00:22:00.661 --> 00:22:02.100
So in particular, data analysis

00:22:02.101 --> 00:22:03.365
problems have a time budget.

00:22:03.366 --> 00:22:05.495
So, you need to be able
to say something like,

00:22:05.496 --> 00:22:08.150
well like in finance
it's microseconds,

00:22:08.151 --> 00:22:12.025
search or Internet
it's milliseconds,

00:22:12.026 --> 00:22:13.765
in medicine maybe it's hours.

00:22:13.766 --> 00:22:17.170
But what there is
always time budget.

00:22:17.171 --> 00:22:20.540
The idea that you control risk
under a time budget is not

00:22:20.541 --> 00:22:21.770
familiar to either
statisticians or

00:22:21.771 --> 00:22:24.645
computer scientists at
a theoretical level, okay?

00:22:24.646 --> 00:22:26.760
So, how do we start to do that?

00:22:26.761 --> 00:22:28.690
So, I think many of

00:22:28.691 --> 00:22:30.290
us who work in
this more theoretical branch

00:22:30.291 --> 00:22:31.800
the field think that
optimization has

00:22:31.801 --> 00:22:33.645
provided the best help with this,

00:22:33.646 --> 00:22:35.060
especially if you're
thinking about

00:22:35.061 --> 00:22:36.905
lower bounds and
complexity results.

00:22:36.906 --> 00:22:39.210
Computer science doesn't have
hardly any lower bounds and

00:22:39.211 --> 00:22:41.485
mostly the ones that you
have are meaningless.

00:22:41.486 --> 00:22:44.170
It's because the computer
science model is too strong.

00:22:44.171 --> 00:22:45.460
Turing complexity is just too

00:22:45.461 --> 00:22:47.205
strong that tried
to do everything,

00:22:47.206 --> 00:22:48.695
when you do not everything

00:22:48.696 --> 00:22:50.235
and we'd get
more meaningful bounds.

00:22:50.236 --> 00:22:51.910
So, authorization tree
does exactly that.

00:22:51.911 --> 00:22:54.220
So, it's provided
algorithms and insights.

00:22:54.221 --> 00:22:57.780
Statistics also has a lot
of good lower bounds.

00:22:57.781 --> 00:22:59.225
I mean, you've
heard of Cramer Rao

00:22:59.226 --> 00:23:00.925
lower bound but there's
a lot of others

00:23:00.926 --> 00:23:02.500
and they have been
very important for

00:23:02.501 --> 00:23:05.060
this older field to progress.

00:23:05.061 --> 00:23:08.105
So, taking statistics lower
bounds and matching them with

00:23:08.106 --> 00:23:09.890
optimization lower bounds
is turning out

00:23:09.891 --> 00:23:12.910
to be very productive.

00:23:12.911 --> 00:23:14.740
Okay, moreover, used to be,

00:23:14.741 --> 00:23:16.170
I learned optimization
in the 80s and it

00:23:16.171 --> 00:23:18.540
was taught as a definished field.

00:23:18.541 --> 00:23:20.110
There was simplex and gradient

00:23:20.111 --> 00:23:21.220
and conjugate gradient,
it was just done.

00:23:21.221 --> 00:23:23.365
There was software for all
of it and it was just done.

00:23:23.366 --> 00:23:26.390
All right. That's turned
out to be completely wrong.

00:23:26.391 --> 00:23:28.570
All the new problems
that we were getting and

00:23:28.571 --> 00:23:30.520
plus the perspectives
that arose out of

00:23:30.521 --> 00:23:33.050
the Soviet Union with

00:23:33.051 --> 00:23:34.630
gradient-based
methods have really

00:23:34.631 --> 00:23:36.030
changed everything. All right.

00:23:36.031 --> 00:23:38.960
So in fact, those of us who
work on this are focussed on

00:23:38.961 --> 00:23:40.595
really simple algorithms
that can work

00:23:40.596 --> 00:23:42.905
in a computer
science scalable sense.

00:23:42.906 --> 00:23:44.860
So, we will restrict
ourselves to gradients,

00:23:44.861 --> 00:23:46.615
no Hessians at least for now.

00:23:46.616 --> 00:23:48.525
That'll probably change.

00:23:48.526 --> 00:23:50.850
We do stochastics because it's

00:23:50.851 --> 00:23:53.695
cheaper and our data
is noisy anyway.

00:23:53.696 --> 00:23:55.350
Then we work on acceleration

00:23:55.351 --> 00:23:57.700
because the geometry matters.

00:23:57.701 --> 00:24:00.400
Acceleration allows you to
bring in geometry and it

00:24:00.401 --> 00:24:01.800
also allows you to achieve some

00:24:01.801 --> 00:24:03.505
of the lower bounds that
I was referring to.

00:24:03.506 --> 00:24:05.240
Then how these things
all interact in

00:24:05.241 --> 00:24:07.770
a detailed way is what
most of us are working on.

00:24:07.771 --> 00:24:09.300
I may acknowledge a
couple of people that

00:24:09.301 --> 00:24:10.700
is Ge Huang who I
met this morning has

00:24:10.701 --> 00:24:12.230
worked a lot on these things and

00:24:12.231 --> 00:24:13.860
Chi Jin sitting in
the front is a student at

00:24:13.861 --> 00:24:15.580
Berkeley who's the expert
and I'm going to be

00:24:15.581 --> 00:24:17.760
talking about some of
his work, with many others.

00:24:17.761 --> 00:24:19.220
This has been a topic
that I think is

00:24:19.221 --> 00:24:21.500
the field of machine learning

00:24:21.501 --> 00:24:22.570
is very very popular these days.

00:24:22.571 --> 00:24:24.070
A lot you'll think
deep learning is everything and

00:24:24.071 --> 00:24:25.850
I have nothing against
deep learning. It's great.

00:24:25.851 --> 00:24:27.500
But at the same time,
there's been

00:24:27.501 --> 00:24:29.540
just as much progress
have done more on

00:24:29.541 --> 00:24:32.225
this theoretical wing
and it's less aware.

00:24:32.226 --> 00:24:34.955
People here and other places
have been doing this.

00:24:34.956 --> 00:24:40.190
Okay, so I learned from Chi
that the bad local minima,

00:24:40.191 --> 00:24:42.420
which I thought were
the core problem

00:24:42.421 --> 00:24:44.255
when he went to
non-convex problems,

00:24:44.256 --> 00:24:46.430
are not so bad after all maybe,

00:24:46.431 --> 00:24:48.650
at least in a wide class
of problems.

00:24:48.651 --> 00:24:50.420
So, there's a lot of
architectures which in

00:24:50.421 --> 00:24:51.630
the last few years
people have proved

00:24:51.631 --> 00:24:53.240
have no local minima at all.

00:24:53.241 --> 00:24:54.935
They have a single global optimum

00:24:54.936 --> 00:24:57.135
and then a ring of
saddle points around that.

00:24:57.136 --> 00:25:00.315
So, it's really saddle points
that are the problem.

00:25:00.316 --> 00:25:02.345
You should think about
a saddle point as

00:25:02.346 --> 00:25:04.725
a lot of directions that
you are coming down but

00:25:04.726 --> 00:25:07.080
then they go back up
again and there's really

00:25:07.081 --> 00:25:08.490
a precious few
directions allow you

00:25:08.491 --> 00:25:09.995
to get out. All right.

00:25:09.996 --> 00:25:10.800
Now, why is that a problem?

00:25:10.801 --> 00:25:12.230
Well, in two three dimensions
it wouldn't be a problem,

00:25:12.231 --> 00:25:14.650
but in 100,000 dimensions
it may be very very hard to

00:25:14.651 --> 00:25:17.790
find that direction
leads you to get out.

00:25:17.791 --> 00:25:19.780
It can take
a huge amount of time.

00:25:19.781 --> 00:25:21.280
In fact, when you
train neural nets,

00:25:21.281 --> 00:25:23.920
you see the error rate comes
down real fast and flattens

00:25:23.921 --> 00:25:25.000
out and then you wait for

00:25:25.001 --> 00:25:26.250
some indeterminate amount of time

00:25:26.251 --> 00:25:27.595
and then it comes down again.

00:25:27.596 --> 00:25:29.560
You're in a saddle point
and you're probably

00:25:29.561 --> 00:25:31.590
hit a couple of
saddle points, right?

00:25:31.591 --> 00:25:33.030
So, we need, if we're
going to really understand

00:25:33.031 --> 00:25:34.470
anything about how long
it's going to take to

00:25:34.471 --> 00:25:37.905
train these things and how
things get larger and larger,

00:25:37.906 --> 00:25:40.250
we need something about
the dimension dependence.

00:25:40.251 --> 00:25:42.790
So, not just rate of convergence,
the classical thing,

00:25:42.791 --> 00:25:44.480
after how many iterations do you

00:25:44.481 --> 00:25:46.495
need to be within
epsilon of the optimum,

00:25:46.496 --> 00:25:50.300
but as a function of
dimension as well.

00:25:50.301 --> 00:25:51.720
Okay, so there's

00:25:51.721 --> 00:25:53.180
a saddle point in
two or three dimensions.

00:25:53.181 --> 00:25:54.190
We're going to be
interested in how do you

00:25:54.191 --> 00:25:55.505
escape it efficiently,

00:25:55.506 --> 00:25:57.395
meaning efficient
computer science sense

00:25:57.396 --> 00:26:00.035
and specifically getting
the dimension dependence.

00:26:00.036 --> 00:26:02.340
Naively, you might think would
be exponentially have to

00:26:02.341 --> 00:26:04.490
search all combinations
of directions, right?

00:26:04.491 --> 00:26:07.775
So, this is work with Chi
and some others Rong Ge,

00:26:07.776 --> 00:26:09.960
Sam Kakade was here in the city,

00:26:09.961 --> 00:26:12.990
and Praneeth Netrapalli who
is at MSR, is that correct?

00:26:12.991 --> 00:26:15.350
Yes and in India.

00:26:15.351 --> 00:26:18.610
Okay, so here's a little bit
of background that led to

00:26:18.611 --> 00:26:22.445
this particular line of work
that I want to focus on.

00:26:22.446 --> 00:26:25.035
In joint work with
Jason Lee and others,

00:26:25.036 --> 00:26:27.110
we were able to show
that gradient descent

00:26:27.111 --> 00:26:29.555
asymptotically will never
end up in a saddle point,

00:26:29.556 --> 00:26:31.180
for anyone's intuition but this

00:26:31.181 --> 00:26:32.800
has been an open
problem actually.

00:26:32.801 --> 00:26:35.210
For gradient flow, which is

00:26:35.211 --> 00:26:38.240
the continuous version
of gradient descent,

00:26:38.241 --> 00:26:39.550
it's the differential equation X

00:26:39.551 --> 00:26:41.130
dot is equal to dot
minus the gradient.

00:26:41.131 --> 00:26:43.600
That's been known for 100 years

00:26:43.601 --> 00:26:46.120
based on Morse theory that it

00:26:46.121 --> 00:26:48.760
asymptotically voids but
that's asymptotics and

00:26:48.761 --> 00:26:51.800
has been open for
the gradient descent,

00:26:51.801 --> 00:26:54.030
that us discretization
gradient flow.

00:26:54.031 --> 00:26:57.910
So, that sounds good but then
there's a negative result,

00:26:57.911 --> 00:27:00.455
with again Chi Jin and Simon Du,

00:27:00.456 --> 00:27:03.260
that gradient descent can

00:27:03.261 --> 00:27:06.305
take exponential time to
avoid saddle points, okay?

00:27:06.306 --> 00:27:08.810
So, there's a class of functions
which aren't that weird,

00:27:08.811 --> 00:27:10.865
where it does take
exponential time.

00:27:10.866 --> 00:27:12.910
So, it's something
of a surprise that

00:27:12.911 --> 00:27:14.830
Chi and some others
proved a theorem

00:27:14.831 --> 00:27:16.705
in 2015 that showed that

00:27:16.706 --> 00:27:18.985
if you add stochasticity
of a certain kind,

00:27:18.986 --> 00:27:23.270
you go from exponential
data polynomial.

00:27:23.271 --> 00:27:26.420
Now, that result still
left us a little bit

00:27:26.421 --> 00:27:27.700
short of the explanation of why

00:27:27.701 --> 00:27:29.095
gradient descent is so effective,

00:27:29.096 --> 00:27:31.250
stochastic gradient so
effective, it's just polynomial.

00:27:31.251 --> 00:27:35.195
It could be high degree
polynomial, okay?

00:27:35.196 --> 00:27:36.520
So, the goal in this line

00:27:36.521 --> 00:27:38.445
of work was to prove
a stronger theorem.

00:27:38.446 --> 00:27:40.650
Okay, so let's get started.

00:27:40.651 --> 00:27:42.375
Let's talk about
convex optimization.

00:27:42.376 --> 00:27:43.460
F is convex.

00:27:43.461 --> 00:27:44.600
Let's look at gradient descent.

00:27:44.601 --> 00:27:47.860
There it is, go
down on a gradient.

00:27:47.861 --> 00:27:50.330
It is a fact that you get

00:27:50.331 --> 00:27:51.650
convergence to global minimum

00:27:51.651 --> 00:27:52.930
under very, very weak conditions.

00:27:52.931 --> 00:27:54.790
Moreover, more importantly
for this talk,

00:27:54.791 --> 00:27:56.310
the number of iterations to

00:27:56.311 --> 00:27:57.995
get there is independent
of the dimension.

00:27:57.996 --> 00:27:59.665
That's less well and appreciated

00:27:59.666 --> 00:28:00.955
and it's an amazing fact.

00:28:00.956 --> 00:28:02.370
You can be at infinite dimensions

00:28:02.371 --> 00:28:03.590
and the number of
iterations to get the

00:28:03.591 --> 00:28:07.230
optimum is the same as if
you're in finite dimensions.

00:28:07.270 --> 00:28:10.490
So, some caveats there but
that's basically true.

00:28:10.491 --> 00:28:16.235
Now, let's go on beyond
convex problems.

00:28:16.236 --> 00:28:18.075
Let's go to the non-convex world
that people like

00:28:18.076 --> 00:28:20.790
Sebastian helped us get into.

00:28:20.791 --> 00:28:23.450
So here, we can
have local maxima,

00:28:23.451 --> 00:28:25.740
we can have saddle points
and local minima.

00:28:25.741 --> 00:28:27.705
So, what can we say
about that world?

00:28:27.706 --> 00:28:29.790
Okay, so first of
all, just briefly,

00:28:29.791 --> 00:28:30.940
this is the list,

00:28:30.941 --> 00:28:32.040
and it's probably gotten longer,

00:28:32.041 --> 00:28:33.400
about non-convex problems that

00:28:33.401 --> 00:28:34.390
have only approval but have

00:28:34.391 --> 00:28:35.970
a single global optimum and they

00:28:35.971 --> 00:28:37.945
have saddle points
is the problem.

00:28:37.946 --> 00:28:39.760
So, an interesting list.

00:28:39.761 --> 00:28:41.130
Doesn't include neural nets but

00:28:41.131 --> 00:28:42.930
it includes lots of

00:28:42.931 --> 00:28:44.950
other interesting and
important problems.

00:28:44.951 --> 00:28:47.980
Okay, so let's just set
up the basics theory,

00:28:47.981 --> 00:28:50.065
the classical case
for gradient case,

00:28:50.066 --> 00:28:52.470
and then move on to
the non-convex case.

00:28:52.471 --> 00:28:54.725
So, for the classical
convex case,

00:28:54.726 --> 00:28:57.830
one talked about a simple
smoothness assumption

00:28:57.831 --> 00:29:00.030
the gradient doesn't change much,

00:29:00.031 --> 00:29:02.550
a minimal assumption
you can make.

00:29:02.551 --> 00:29:05.050
Then you attempted to

00:29:05.051 --> 00:29:06.310
prove that algorithm
would converge

00:29:06.311 --> 00:29:07.785
to a first-order
stationary point.

00:29:07.786 --> 00:29:09.460
So, that would be a point

00:29:09.461 --> 00:29:10.695
where the gradient
is equal to zero,

00:29:10.696 --> 00:29:12.805
classical stationary
point from calculus.

00:29:12.806 --> 00:29:14.500
Then we put a little
epsilon ball around

00:29:14.501 --> 00:29:16.800
that so that we can get a rate.

00:29:16.801 --> 00:29:18.340
So, you could do it for
different values of epsilon.

00:29:18.341 --> 00:29:19.270
You can talk about the rate at

00:29:19.271 --> 00:29:20.325
which you're approaching
the optimum.

00:29:20.326 --> 00:29:21.675
That's why the epsilon is there.

00:29:21.676 --> 00:29:23.680
So, it's called the epsilon
first-order stationary point.

00:29:23.681 --> 00:29:26.530
Here is an example
of a result due to

00:29:26.531 --> 00:29:28.420
Nesterov which shows that

00:29:28.421 --> 00:29:31.285
the convergence rate is
one over epsilon squared,

00:29:31.286 --> 00:29:33.490
which is polynomial rate.

00:29:33.491 --> 00:29:35.620
It says, if you want
a really small epsilon,

00:29:35.621 --> 00:29:37.375
the number of iterations
is going to be

00:29:37.376 --> 00:29:40.220
more. It's going to grow.

00:29:40.420 --> 00:29:43.160
This is the initial
distance to the

00:29:43.161 --> 00:29:45.150
optimum and that makes sense.

00:29:45.151 --> 00:29:46.560
If you're far away,
you got to get there.

00:29:46.561 --> 00:29:47.935
It's going to take
that amount of time.

00:29:47.936 --> 00:29:49.470
There's your Lipschitz constant

00:29:49.471 --> 00:29:51.000
and no dimension dependence.

00:29:51.001 --> 00:29:52.850
That's the amazing thing about

00:29:52.851 --> 00:29:55.560
this algorithm,
no dimension dependence.

00:29:55.561 --> 00:29:58.215
Okay, what about
going beyond that.

00:29:58.216 --> 00:30:00.370
So, let's go now to
the non-convex case.

00:30:00.371 --> 00:30:02.370
So first-order
stationary is no longer

00:30:02.371 --> 00:30:03.860
an appropriate criterion because

00:30:03.861 --> 00:30:05.695
saddle points are
first-order stationary.

00:30:05.696 --> 00:30:07.305
We don't want to
hit saddle points.

00:30:07.306 --> 00:30:09.060
So, we need to go beyond that.

00:30:09.061 --> 00:30:11.715
Let's define a notion of
secondary stationarity,

00:30:11.716 --> 00:30:13.330
where the gradient is again

00:30:13.331 --> 00:30:16.045
zero or you put a ball
around that to get a rate.

00:30:16.046 --> 00:30:18.360
The minimum eigenvalue is
greater or equal to zero,

00:30:18.361 --> 00:30:19.800
but again you let a little slope,

00:30:19.801 --> 00:30:22.140
you let it go slightly negative
so you can get a rate.

00:30:22.141 --> 00:30:23.850
This is a particular
parameterization

00:30:23.851 --> 00:30:25.540
of that amount of negativity.

00:30:25.541 --> 00:30:27.085
That row is a Lipschitz constant.

00:30:27.086 --> 00:30:28.990
Now, for the Hessian, we need

00:30:28.991 --> 00:30:31.000
to make a little smoother
assumption also in the Hessian,

00:30:31.001 --> 00:30:33.015
because the Hessian
changes wildly.

00:30:33.016 --> 00:30:36.240
You're not going to be
able to converge out

00:30:36.241 --> 00:30:39.500
of a saddle point.

00:30:39.501 --> 00:30:41.860
Okay, so this is the original
paper doing this.

00:30:41.861 --> 00:30:44.215
There has been
some work since then,

00:30:44.216 --> 00:30:49.350
but this is a minimal algorithm,

00:30:49.351 --> 00:30:51.805
a simple algorithm that can
be analyzed theoretically.

00:30:51.806 --> 00:30:53.880
So, it's just gradient
descent but

00:30:53.881 --> 00:30:56.375
from time to time
has sporadically,

00:30:56.376 --> 00:30:58.390
you add uniform noise

00:30:58.391 --> 00:31:00.140
from a ball around
your current iterate.

00:31:00.141 --> 00:31:03.130
You do that when
the gradient is small,

00:31:03.131 --> 00:31:05.275
but you don't do
it too frequently.

00:31:05.276 --> 00:31:07.350
There's a parameter T
which says you only

00:31:07.351 --> 00:31:09.890
do it once every T steps.

00:31:09.891 --> 00:31:12.185
This can be removed.

00:31:12.186 --> 00:31:17.040
The particular choice of
uniform ball is not essential,

00:31:17.041 --> 00:31:19.900
but the basic idea
of having to have

00:31:19.901 --> 00:31:21.860
stochasticity is
certainly necessary.

00:31:21.861 --> 00:31:24.235
We saw the exponential
rate before.

00:31:24.236 --> 00:31:26.430
This is just an example
of an algorithm

00:31:26.431 --> 00:31:28.580
can be analyzed
relatively simply.

00:31:28.581 --> 00:31:31.885
Okay, so here is the theorem

00:31:31.886 --> 00:31:35.275
from this work by Chi Jin et al.

00:31:35.276 --> 00:31:37.970
It saw the number of
iterations done in

00:31:37.971 --> 00:31:39.070
all these assumptions to find

00:31:39.071 --> 00:31:41.275
a second-order stationary point
now, not first order.

00:31:41.276 --> 00:31:43.350
It looks exactly the same
as the Nesterov result.

00:31:43.351 --> 00:31:44.880
So, one over epsilon squared

00:31:44.881 --> 00:31:46.790
distance the optimum
Lipschitz constant.

00:31:46.791 --> 00:31:48.780
The only difference is that
little twiddle right there,

00:31:48.781 --> 00:31:50.570
which is standard theoreticians

00:31:50.571 --> 00:31:52.425
trick for hiding
the dimension dependence.

00:31:52.426 --> 00:31:53.830
But we don't want to hide
the dimension dependence.

00:31:53.831 --> 00:31:55.090
So, the whole point of
this talk is that we're

00:31:55.091 --> 00:31:57.065
being honest about
it and there it is.

00:31:57.066 --> 00:31:59.430
It's polylogarithmic
in dimension.

00:31:59.431 --> 00:32:00.650
So, it is not polynomial.

00:32:00.651 --> 00:32:01.860
It's way better than that.

00:32:01.861 --> 00:32:05.020
It's logarithmic
effectively, okay?

00:32:05.021 --> 00:32:06.460
That four is probably

00:32:06.461 --> 00:32:08.280
an artifact of
a predictor proof technique,

00:32:08.281 --> 00:32:10.660
but it's probably logarithmic as

00:32:10.661 --> 00:32:13.840
the truth but it is logarithmic.

00:32:13.841 --> 00:32:16.370
So, that I think
is perhaps one of

00:32:16.371 --> 00:32:19.580
the first convincing reasons

00:32:19.581 --> 00:32:21.080
why you should think stochastic

00:32:21.081 --> 00:32:22.160
gradient should work well in

00:32:22.161 --> 00:32:23.230
high dimensions because

00:32:23.231 --> 00:32:26.180
its dimension dependence
is so favorable.

00:32:26.181 --> 00:32:28.970
The proof technique

00:32:28.971 --> 00:32:30.510
here basically involves in

00:32:30.511 --> 00:32:32.095
some sense some
difference in geometry.

00:32:32.096 --> 00:32:33.600
Around saddle point there is

00:32:33.601 --> 00:32:35.550
a region that has
seen its variants.

00:32:35.551 --> 00:32:37.500
So, there is a region where if

00:32:37.501 --> 00:32:39.630
you're in that region, it's
hard to get out of it.

00:32:39.631 --> 00:32:41.070
The flow due to

00:32:41.071 --> 00:32:43.585
the negative eigenvalue
doesn't hit you fast enough,

00:32:43.586 --> 00:32:45.785
and you're sort of
stuck for a long time.

00:32:45.786 --> 00:32:48.120
So, you need to measure
the width of this region,

00:32:48.121 --> 00:32:49.370
and this paper used

00:32:49.371 --> 00:32:51.500
a coupling technique
from probability theory.

00:32:51.501 --> 00:32:53.400
It's said, I don't
know that width

00:32:53.401 --> 00:32:55.845
but I started
two coupled diffusions.

00:32:55.846 --> 00:32:59.080
They're width r apart and
then vary r until you get to

00:32:59.081 --> 00:33:00.580
phase transitions when they

00:33:00.581 --> 00:33:02.575
start to fly apart
from each other.

00:33:02.576 --> 00:33:03.820
That point you know the width.

00:33:03.821 --> 00:33:05.530
So, you don't just
have to do kind

00:33:05.531 --> 00:33:07.545
of replace it with a flat slab,

00:33:07.546 --> 00:33:09.510
which had been done in
the previous work to get

00:33:09.511 --> 00:33:10.855
the polynomial dependence and

00:33:10.856 --> 00:33:13.135
this logarithm that comes
out of that argument.

00:33:13.136 --> 00:33:15.700
Okay.

00:33:15.701 --> 00:33:17.445
All right. So, with
that as background,

00:33:17.446 --> 00:33:21.530
there's a few key ideas
that emerged in this,

00:33:21.531 --> 00:33:24.040
that are going to be allowing

00:33:24.041 --> 00:33:25.080
us to look at some other problem.

00:33:25.081 --> 00:33:26.325
So, first of all, I
haven't said anything

00:33:26.326 --> 00:33:27.905
yet about acceleration.

00:33:27.906 --> 00:33:30.680
Again, there're
some real experts in the room,

00:33:30.681 --> 00:33:33.480
and they will tell you

00:33:33.481 --> 00:33:35.360
accelerations pretty important
if you're a theoretician.

00:33:35.361 --> 00:33:37.115
It adds momentum to the problem,

00:33:37.116 --> 00:33:39.415
it allows you to achieve
some of these Oracle rates,

00:33:39.416 --> 00:33:41.580
and we want to

00:33:41.581 --> 00:33:43.340
bring it into contact
with these other ideas.

00:33:43.341 --> 00:33:45.440
So, there's been an open problem.

00:33:45.441 --> 00:33:46.790
If I'm arriving at a saddle

00:33:46.791 --> 00:33:47.980
point is it possible to kind of

00:33:47.981 --> 00:33:49.360
blase pass the saddle point

00:33:49.361 --> 00:33:50.970
because of the
momentum I built up?

00:33:50.971 --> 00:33:52.680
Or is momentum not independent?

00:33:52.681 --> 00:33:54.685
So, it did not help me
with saddle points.

00:33:54.686 --> 00:33:56.440
Can we use other kinds
of stochastic

00:33:56.441 --> 00:33:57.820
models not just this one?

00:33:57.821 --> 00:34:01.425
If we go to kind of fold
stochastic models we talk about

00:34:01.426 --> 00:34:03.840
diffusions and how the diffusions

00:34:03.841 --> 00:34:04.670
interact with the kind of

00:34:04.671 --> 00:34:06.345
this convex and
non-convex geometry.

00:34:06.346 --> 00:34:09.985
So, all kinds of new problems
we'll start to look at.

00:34:09.986 --> 00:34:14.295
In my group, we have
gotten a lot of help by

00:34:14.296 --> 00:34:16.055
thinking about
these problems not in

00:34:16.056 --> 00:34:18.310
discrete time but
in continuous time.

00:34:18.311 --> 00:34:20.070
So, that's probably
the big message

00:34:20.071 --> 00:34:21.825
of my talk, it's that,

00:34:21.826 --> 00:34:24.340
if you thought you could avoid
differential equations and

00:34:24.341 --> 00:34:25.390
you went into computer science to

00:34:25.391 --> 00:34:26.900
avoid differential equations,

00:34:26.901 --> 00:34:28.630
I'm sorry to tell
you you're going to

00:34:28.631 --> 00:34:30.400
have to learn about
differential equations.

00:34:30.401 --> 00:34:33.990
All right. Now, why? Well, we're

00:34:33.991 --> 00:34:35.720
trying to develop
fast algorithms,

00:34:35.721 --> 00:34:38.010
and algorithms we know
how fast they can be,

00:34:38.011 --> 00:34:41.130
and what we want to analyze
what's the fastest rate?

00:34:41.131 --> 00:34:43.650
So, what does it mean to have
the speed of an algorithm?

00:34:43.651 --> 00:34:45.890
If I'm just hopping along
some discrete points,

00:34:45.891 --> 00:34:49.260
what does it mean to hop faster
on those discrete points?

00:34:49.261 --> 00:34:50.740
Well, it means you've
embedded the problem in

00:34:50.741 --> 00:34:53.215
a continuum of time somehow.

00:34:53.216 --> 00:34:54.400
All right.

00:34:54.401 --> 00:34:56.400
If you go to bed the problem
of continuum of time,

00:34:56.401 --> 00:34:59.430
you have to start talking
about differential equations.

00:34:59.930 --> 00:35:02.340
So, this line of
work has done that.

00:35:02.341 --> 00:35:03.750
It's gone pretty
far at this point.

00:35:03.751 --> 00:35:05.600
So, I'll tell you a little bit
about Variational,

00:35:05.601 --> 00:35:07.865
Hamiltonian Symplectic
Perspectives.

00:35:07.866 --> 00:35:09.260
Andrew Wibisono is now

00:35:09.261 --> 00:35:10.630
a postdoc but he was
a student with me,

00:35:10.631 --> 00:35:12.955
and Ashia is still the student
with me one more year.

00:35:12.956 --> 00:35:18.650
They played a major role
in developing this work,

00:35:18.651 --> 00:35:20.240
the Variational,
Hamiltonian framework.

00:35:20.241 --> 00:35:22.060
Then Ashia has gone
quite a bit further with

00:35:22.061 --> 00:35:24.595
Lyapunov perspectives
on all of this stuff.

00:35:24.596 --> 00:35:26.120
Then on the Symplectic work,

00:35:26.121 --> 00:35:29.840
Mike Betancourt has joined us
from Colombia to help out.

00:35:30.250 --> 00:35:32.705
So, this is about embedding

00:35:32.706 --> 00:35:34.540
optimization problems
in continuous time,

00:35:34.541 --> 00:35:35.795
and once you've done that,

00:35:35.796 --> 00:35:37.615
you learn about why

00:35:37.616 --> 00:35:39.690
algorithms would behave
certain ways in discrete time.

00:35:39.691 --> 00:35:42.590
There's going to be phase
transitions happening only in

00:35:42.591 --> 00:35:44.320
continuous time that you

00:35:44.321 --> 00:35:45.960
would never have seen
just in discrete time.

00:35:45.961 --> 00:35:48.350
Or with great labor.

00:35:48.351 --> 00:35:50.470
So, let me say
something a little bit,

00:35:50.471 --> 00:35:53.545
kind of grandiose sounding,

00:35:53.546 --> 00:35:55.570
but if you look at
the fields that are

00:35:55.571 --> 00:35:57.045
mathematically the most mature,

00:35:57.046 --> 00:35:58.630
they make serious usage

00:35:58.631 --> 00:36:00.275
of the fundamental
theorem of calculus.

00:36:00.276 --> 00:36:01.695
There are some differentials,

00:36:01.696 --> 00:36:03.240
and there's also some integrals.

00:36:03.241 --> 00:36:04.920
All right. Fields that aren't so

00:36:04.921 --> 00:36:07.185
mathematically surely, I
picked one or the other.

00:36:07.186 --> 00:36:09.740
So, physics is
the poster child for this,

00:36:09.741 --> 00:36:11.620
Newton wrote down
F equals MA which

00:36:11.621 --> 00:36:13.785
is laws of nature
using derivatives.

00:36:13.786 --> 00:36:15.940
So, differential
equation, and then

00:36:15.941 --> 00:36:18.250
LeBrons came around about
100 years later and said,

00:36:18.251 --> 00:36:20.320
"I can rewrite all that physics
using integrals."

00:36:20.321 --> 00:36:21.480
I'm going to integrate

00:36:21.481 --> 00:36:23.980
an energy and then moreover
he introduced optimization.

00:36:23.981 --> 00:36:25.790
I'm going to optimize
the integral of

00:36:25.791 --> 00:36:29.330
the energy and I will get
out the same physics.

00:36:30.080 --> 00:36:32.440
Probability theory is full of

00:36:32.441 --> 00:36:36.175
this telegram smart paths or

00:36:36.176 --> 00:36:39.790
this Feynman diagrams
or this statistics

00:36:39.791 --> 00:36:42.840
as the Laplace expansions,
saddlepoint expansions.

00:36:42.841 --> 00:36:44.780
So, anyway, all of this is
really just fundamental from

00:36:44.781 --> 00:36:46.680
calculus used in elaborated ways.

00:36:46.681 --> 00:36:48.920
The numerical disciplines
also finite element analysis,

00:36:48.921 --> 00:36:50.630
is you take a PDE and
you've just created

00:36:50.631 --> 00:36:52.205
little patches using integrals

00:36:52.206 --> 00:36:54.095
and relate those integrals
to each other.

00:36:54.096 --> 00:36:57.020
Monte Carlo is trying to
do integration by using

00:36:57.021 --> 00:36:59.885
derivatives inside of
the integral, and so on.

00:36:59.886 --> 00:37:01.640
So, you can now ask
about optimization is.

00:37:01.641 --> 00:37:03.195
Is it one of these mature fields?

00:37:03.196 --> 00:37:04.600
As I alluded to earlier,

00:37:04.601 --> 00:37:06.750
it was kind of thought
of as done.

00:37:07.580 --> 00:37:09.690
So, it's not done,

00:37:09.691 --> 00:37:12.865
and in particular, it's only
focused on differentiation.

00:37:12.866 --> 00:37:14.550
You open up paper after paper,

00:37:14.551 --> 00:37:15.770
and there's
all these derivatives,

00:37:15.771 --> 00:37:19.120
and there's inequalities
based on the derivatives,

00:37:19.121 --> 00:37:20.875
but there's almost
never an integral sign.

00:37:20.876 --> 00:37:23.255
Just some Taylor expansion of
something you're ignoring,

00:37:23.256 --> 00:37:24.445
but otherwise it's not really

00:37:24.446 --> 00:37:25.855
fundamentally part of the field.

00:37:25.856 --> 00:37:29.020
So, I would argue that
field is not mature yet.

00:37:29.200 --> 00:37:32.360
So, let's now again

00:37:32.361 --> 00:37:34.590
fix ideas by looking
at a simple situation.

00:37:34.591 --> 00:37:36.200
Let's talk about
convex optimization

00:37:36.201 --> 00:37:37.860
again, smooth convex
optimization.

00:37:37.861 --> 00:37:39.270
Here's gradient descent again.

00:37:39.271 --> 00:37:41.325
Now, here's a new fact,

00:37:41.326 --> 00:37:44.410
that if you're a statistician,
otherwise you might not.

00:37:44.411 --> 00:37:46.880
It's that not only does
this algorithm have

00:37:46.881 --> 00:37:50.070
this specific convergence rate
for a particular function,

00:37:50.071 --> 00:37:52.160
over the entire class
of convex functions,

00:37:52.161 --> 00:37:53.375
it has a convergence rate.

00:37:53.376 --> 00:37:54.685
That rate is here.

00:37:54.686 --> 00:37:56.355
So, this is the worst case rate.

00:37:56.356 --> 00:37:58.640
It says that after k
steps have gone by,

00:37:58.641 --> 00:38:01.030
you're within one over k order,

00:38:01.031 --> 00:38:03.160
one over k of the optimum.

00:38:03.161 --> 00:38:04.910
So, it's a convergence rate for

00:38:04.911 --> 00:38:07.195
the transit complexity
results really.

00:38:07.196 --> 00:38:09.280
So, once that was known,

00:38:09.281 --> 00:38:11.020
and it's probably been
a few decades now.

00:38:11.021 --> 00:38:12.400
The Russian school our

00:38:12.401 --> 00:38:15.345
[inaudible] asks a
very interesting question.

00:38:15.346 --> 00:38:18.700
They said, is there a general
complexity result here?

00:38:18.701 --> 00:38:20.160
Is there a lower bound for

00:38:20.161 --> 00:38:22.820
this class of algorithms?
Now, what does that mean?

00:38:22.821 --> 00:38:24.605
Well, first of all,
he had to think about

00:38:24.606 --> 00:38:25.740
what kind of computer am I

00:38:25.741 --> 00:38:27.445
talking about to
get a lower bound.

00:38:27.446 --> 00:38:29.050
So, when we look at
all possible algorithms,

00:38:29.051 --> 00:38:30.865
we'd like to talk
about the platform.

00:38:30.866 --> 00:38:32.640
If you talked about
Turing complexity

00:38:32.641 --> 00:38:34.245
this point, that wouldn't
be very helpful.

00:38:34.246 --> 00:38:35.770
The lower boundary is a zero.

00:38:35.771 --> 00:38:37.000
You just look at
the description of

00:38:37.001 --> 00:38:38.820
the function and
figure out the answer.

00:38:38.821 --> 00:38:41.485
So, it had to be more
restricted to family,

00:38:41.486 --> 00:38:42.570
and so he said, "Well,
I'm just going to

00:38:42.571 --> 00:38:44.120
allow you to look
at the gradient,

00:38:44.121 --> 00:38:45.160
and I'm going to
allow you to keep

00:38:45.161 --> 00:38:46.450
all previous gradients and

00:38:46.451 --> 00:38:47.750
your updates must be

00:38:47.751 --> 00:38:50.530
in the linear span of
all previous gradients."

00:38:50.531 --> 00:38:53.140
So, it's a very powerful machine

00:38:53.141 --> 00:38:54.530
but it's not Turing-complete,

00:38:54.531 --> 00:38:56.900
but you can make
progress with it.

00:38:56.901 --> 00:38:59.815
So, after doing that he
said, "In this family,

00:38:59.816 --> 00:39:01.650
for a smooth convex functions,

00:39:01.651 --> 00:39:03.120
what's the best you can do?"

00:39:03.121 --> 00:39:05.350
He proved that it was
one over k squared.

00:39:05.351 --> 00:39:07.540
It was faster than
gradient descent.

00:39:07.541 --> 00:39:10.995
So, there was an open
period of time where

00:39:10.996 --> 00:39:12.580
no one knew whether it was

00:39:12.581 --> 00:39:13.840
an algorithm that could
be gradient descent

00:39:13.841 --> 00:39:15.370
and arrive at that lower bound.

00:39:15.371 --> 00:39:18.720
So, it was something of
a surprise when Yuri Nesterov in

00:39:18.721 --> 00:39:21.550
1983 wrote down this algorithm

00:39:21.551 --> 00:39:24.090
which has got two pieces
instead of just one,

00:39:24.091 --> 00:39:28.775
and it achieves the lower bound
of one over k squared.

00:39:28.776 --> 00:39:31.040
So, it's kind of
some amazing facts about,

00:39:31.041 --> 00:39:31.600
well, first of all,

00:39:31.601 --> 00:39:34.200
it is kind of a gradient
descent thing, the y-variable.

00:39:34.201 --> 00:39:38.105
There's two variables now,
y is using the x gradient,

00:39:38.106 --> 00:39:40.520
and then the new x is kind

00:39:40.521 --> 00:39:44.070
of a linear combination
of the previous two y's.

00:39:44.071 --> 00:39:46.500
So, it's got this heavy ball
or momentum flavor,

00:39:46.501 --> 00:39:49.330
there's like a little bit
of implicit here,

00:39:49.331 --> 00:39:52.210
but it's a bit of a mystery
where this comes from,

00:39:52.211 --> 00:39:54.900
but it's no mystery that
it does achieve this rate.

00:39:54.901 --> 00:39:56.605
That's really amazing.

00:39:56.606 --> 00:39:58.130
Moreover, one of the mysteries

00:39:58.131 --> 00:39:59.965
is that this thing is
not a descent algorithm,

00:39:59.966 --> 00:40:01.150
it can go down and then go

00:40:01.151 --> 00:40:02.670
back up, and go back down again.

00:40:02.671 --> 00:40:04.160
That seems strange if
you're trying to get

