It's amazing quite
that they asked

00:49:47.671 --> 00:49:49.730
this question and
they answered it.

00:49:49.731 --> 00:49:52.190
So, this area seems like
integration does this,

00:49:52.191 --> 00:49:55.995
and so I've spent a year
reading all about this.

00:49:55.996 --> 00:49:58.240
Here's the basic
short version of this

00:49:58.241 --> 00:50:00.755
that normally you think
of a vector field,

00:50:00.756 --> 00:50:02.485
a differential equation
of the vector field.

00:50:02.486 --> 00:50:04.280
I'm moving along, I'm at a point,

00:50:04.281 --> 00:50:05.430
vector is leading that point

00:50:05.431 --> 00:50:06.640
and I follow that
vector a little bit,

00:50:06.641 --> 00:50:09.195
then I got the next vector
and I stitch this long.

00:50:09.196 --> 00:50:11.090
Symplectic integration has
different perspective,

00:50:11.091 --> 00:50:12.190
it says at any given point,

00:50:12.191 --> 00:50:14.270
there's a triple of
vectors and that defines

00:50:14.271 --> 00:50:16.520
a little volume element and
as I move along the flow,

00:50:16.521 --> 00:50:18.075
that volume that will shear.

00:50:18.076 --> 00:50:20.920
But, I want the actual volume
stays constant.

00:50:20.921 --> 00:50:23.200
Can I do that? Well, the

00:50:23.201 --> 00:50:25.790
volumes that little determinates
and alternating form.

00:50:25.791 --> 00:50:28.175
So, I have now alternating
forms on a manifold,

00:50:28.176 --> 00:50:30.360
and Poincare' already
worked on that.

00:50:30.361 --> 00:50:31.990
Turns out you can do it, you can

00:50:31.991 --> 00:50:34.410
exactly conserve
these little volume elements

00:50:34.411 --> 00:50:35.780
and the integrators that do

00:50:35.781 --> 00:50:38.620
this are not
Runge-Kutta or Euler,

00:50:38.621 --> 00:50:42.420
or whatever, they are a mixture
of implicit and explicit.

00:50:42.421 --> 00:50:45.140
So, it's implicit
Runge-Kutta explicit.

00:50:45.141 --> 00:50:47.100
If you do one or the other,
you're not symplectic,

00:50:47.101 --> 00:50:47.870
but if you mix them in

00:50:47.871 --> 00:50:49.690
a particular way,
you are symplectic.

00:50:49.691 --> 00:50:52.130
Meaning, that you can
serve these quantities.

00:50:52.131 --> 00:50:53.650
Now, this seems pretty far away

00:50:53.651 --> 00:50:55.680
from acceleration and
certainly very far

00:50:55.681 --> 00:50:59.105
away from AI, but it's not.

00:50:59.106 --> 00:51:01.710
So, when you conserve
these quantities,

00:51:01.711 --> 00:51:03.030
an additional benefit is

00:51:03.031 --> 00:51:05.580
that a bunch of
higher-order terms vanish.

00:51:05.581 --> 00:51:07.350
Because, it's symmetries
are being conserved.

00:51:07.351 --> 00:51:09.180
That means you can
move much faster.

00:51:09.181 --> 00:51:10.755
You can take
much bigger step-sizes

00:51:10.756 --> 00:51:12.390
and that's the connection
to acceleration.

00:51:12.391 --> 00:51:16.280
Take much faster step size,

00:51:16.281 --> 00:51:19.195
even optimal size
steps, all right?

00:51:19.196 --> 00:51:23.175
So, long story short,

00:51:23.176 --> 00:51:24.910
now there's a
mathematical recipe that

00:51:24.911 --> 00:51:26.735
starts from an
optimization problem,

00:51:26.736 --> 00:51:28.840
and writes down
the Bregman Lagrangian.

00:51:28.841 --> 00:51:31.210
Now, we're going to go into
the Hamiltonian formalism

00:51:31.211 --> 00:51:32.090
which is the easier way

00:51:32.091 --> 00:51:33.540
to develop symplectic
integrators,

00:51:33.541 --> 00:51:35.020
sort of the standard
physics recipe.

00:51:35.021 --> 00:51:36.825
You take a Lagrange, you
do the Legendre transform,

00:51:36.826 --> 00:51:39.165
conju-duality goes
over to Hamiltonian.

00:51:39.166 --> 00:51:40.815
This Hamiltonian is time varying.

00:51:40.816 --> 00:51:42.220
We want to get a time-invariant,

00:51:42.221 --> 00:51:43.260
one because that's
the easiest way to

00:51:43.261 --> 00:51:44.590
develop symplectic integrators.

00:51:44.591 --> 00:51:46.425
So, we do a standard procedure
called "lifting".

00:51:46.426 --> 00:51:49.205
We introduce a time variable
and its conjugate dual,

00:51:49.206 --> 00:51:51.285
put them in as
explicit state variables.

00:51:51.286 --> 00:51:52.620
Now, we're on a space in which

00:51:52.621 --> 00:51:54.415
the Hamiltonian is
time-invariant,

00:51:54.416 --> 00:51:55.680
and you just run a symplectic

00:51:55.681 --> 00:51:57.225
integrator on that off the shelf.

00:51:57.226 --> 00:51:58.870
People in real physics that's

00:51:58.871 --> 00:52:00.230
similar to cosmos or proteins,

00:52:00.231 --> 00:52:01.800
whatever, only use
symplectic integrators.

00:52:01.801 --> 00:52:02.460
They do not use anything else.

00:52:02.461 --> 00:52:03.720
So, there are
standard software or

00:52:03.721 --> 00:52:05.965
standard methodology
for doing this.

00:52:05.966 --> 00:52:08.350
Okay, so let me show you
a picture of doing this.

00:52:08.351 --> 00:52:10.120
This is just a particular
high dimensional

00:52:10.121 --> 00:52:12.260
optimization problem,
particular function.

00:52:12.261 --> 00:52:14.710
We didn't have to think at all.

00:52:14.711 --> 00:52:16.460
We simply plugged
in the function,

00:52:16.461 --> 00:52:17.665
Euler-Lagrange equation,

00:52:17.666 --> 00:52:20.005
Hamiltonian selecting her,
and putting it on a computer,

00:52:20.006 --> 00:52:23.290
and you see it converges.
It is oscillating.

00:52:23.291 --> 00:52:24.980
It goes up and down as

00:52:24.981 --> 00:52:28.460
predicted and we are
achieving an oracle rate.

00:52:28.461 --> 00:52:29.740
Okay.

00:52:29.741 --> 00:52:33.340
If I didn't want to spend
much more time on this,

00:52:33.341 --> 00:52:35.230
but if I put Nesterov
next to this,

00:52:35.231 --> 00:52:37.370
Nesterov roughly is the same.

00:52:37.371 --> 00:52:38.850
It has a phase near the

00:52:38.851 --> 00:52:39.920
end which it goes
a little faster,

00:52:39.921 --> 00:52:41.850
which I could talk more
about if you are interested.

00:52:41.851 --> 00:52:43.820
But now, if you turn
up the step size,

00:52:43.821 --> 00:52:45.310
Nesterov goes unstable,

00:52:45.311 --> 00:52:48.885
and this thing stay stable
down to a factor of about 10.

00:52:48.886 --> 00:52:50.540
We can get a 10 wall-clock

00:52:50.541 --> 00:52:52.685
improvement with this algorithm.

00:52:52.686 --> 00:52:56.005
Okay. Now, I'm going to go
back to work with Chi again.

00:52:56.006 --> 00:52:57.550
Now that we have
some ingredients,

00:52:57.551 --> 00:53:00.370
we can put these
together. Yeah, please.

00:53:00.371 --> 00:53:03.960
&gt;&gt; You mentioned, so implicit
and explicit, things.

00:53:03.961 --> 00:53:04.470
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:53:04.471 --> 00:53:08.150
&gt;&gt; So, computationally if you
should confuse [inaudible].

00:53:08.151 --> 00:53:10.340
&gt;&gt; Yeah. So, you do
what's called splitting.

00:53:10.341 --> 00:53:12.160
You take this big
old Hamiltonian,

00:53:12.161 --> 00:53:13.185
you split it into pieces,

00:53:13.186 --> 00:53:16.385
and you make sure
the implicit part is easy to do.

00:53:16.386 --> 00:53:17.460
Typically, this what's called

00:53:17.461 --> 00:53:18.780
the "leapfrog integrator" with

00:53:18.781 --> 00:53:21.635
the implicit part is
actually not so complicated.

00:53:21.636 --> 00:53:23.140
But, absolutely if
you split wrong,

00:53:23.141 --> 00:53:26.230
you can have the implicit part
be a load, okay?

00:53:26.231 --> 00:53:27.810
But, it's part of
future research to kind of

00:53:27.811 --> 00:53:30.105
characterize cases where
that's not the problem.

00:53:30.106 --> 00:53:31.950
Okay. So, I'm going to try to

00:53:31.951 --> 00:53:33.800
finish up here in a
couple of minutes,

00:53:33.801 --> 00:53:36.295
sliding into the optima here.

